$\begin{align}
\left(x^2\right)^{4}&=x^{2\cdot 4}
\\\\
&=x^{8}
\end{align}$

This follows from the general rule $\left(x^m\right)^{n}=x^{m\cdot n}$.

We can also see this is correct by expanding the powers.

$\begin{align}
\left(x^2\right)^{4}&=\underbrace{x^2\cdot x^2\cdot x^2\cdot x^2}_\text{4 times}
\\\\\\
&=\underbrace{
\underbrace{x\cdot x}_\text{2 times}
\cdot \underbrace{x\cdot x}_\text{2 times}
\cdot \underbrace{x\cdot x}_\text{2 times}
\cdot \underbrace{x\cdot x}_\text{2 times}}
_\text{4 times}
\\\\
&=x^{8}
\end{align}$
